  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">IN BEST HYDRATE</p>
    <h2>Why sodium bicarbonate?</h2>
    <p>It contributes <strong>sodium</strong>, an important extracellular electrolyte, and <strong>bicarbonate</strong>, a physiologically familiar extracellular anion.</p>
    <p>It also gives the prepared drink a different ionic and acid-base profile than sodium chloride alone.</p>
    <p><strong>That is the Bicarb Bonus™.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">SAME INGREDIENT, DIFFERENT USE</p>
  <h2>Hydration is not bicarbonate loading.</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>In the body</h3><p>Bicarbonate is continuously produced, transported and regulated as part of normal physiology.</p></article>
    <article><h3>In hydration</h3><p>Sodium bicarbonate can contribute sodium + bicarbonate as one part of an electrolyte formula.</p></article>
    <article><h3>In loading protocols</h3><p>Sports studies often use much larger acute doses, commonly around 0.2–0.5 g/kg body mass.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">PERFORMANCE RESEARCH</p>
    <h2>High-dose bicarbonate has real sports research behind it.</h2>
    <p>Position statements and controlled studies support performance effects in some high-intensity exercise settings. Gastrointestinal side effects are also well documented and vary with dose, timing and delivery.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate does not claim those high-dose effects for its hydration serving. The useful takeaway is simpler: bicarbonate is a legitimate physiological ion with a real place in formulation science.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">ORAL HYDRATION HISTORY</p>
    <h2>Bicarbonate has a long hydration history.</h2>
    <p>Earlier oral rehydration formulations used sodium bicarbonate. Modern World Health Organization reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts use trisodium citrate, in part because citrate is more stable in storage.</p>
    <p>That history is one reason bicarbonate remains relevant to hydration chemistry today.</p>
  </div>
</section>

